Run the worker pools through concurrent.futures.

A multiprocessing pool cannot be shut down once one of its workers has died. A
worker waiting for its next task holds the task queue's read lock, a POSIX
semaphore, so a worker killed there leaves that lock held for good. Every other
worker then waits for the lock, and both close() and terminate() wait for those
workers, with no timeout. The underlying problem is
https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/66587, open since 2014.

concurrent.futures reports a worker that died instead of waiting for it, so gyp
fails and says why:

 Exception: A process in the process pool was terminated abruptly while the
 future was running or pending.

It also starts a worker per submitted task rather than the full set up front, so
a machine reporting 384 cores no longer gets 384 processes to read 73 files.

